# Gradient descent, by hand

Almost every model you have ever trained was fit the same way: start somewhere,
look at which way is downhill, take a step, repeat. That is **gradient descent**,
and for all its fame it is small enough to watch happen. This page lets you do
exactly that. Change the step size, add some momentum, drag the starting point
around the slope, and see where the little ball rolls.

We will descend a single, deliberately awkward surface throughout: a long, narrow
valley. It is the example that shows why the step size is a knife-edge and why
momentum was invented, and it is simple enough that we can write its slope down by
hand in a moment.

## Play with it first {#sec-playground}

Here is the whole story in one picture. The rings are contours of the loss, low in
the middle and high at the edges. The dot is where descent **starts**, and the line
is the path it takes. Drag the dot anywhere on the slope; nudge the three sliders.
Push the step size high enough and watch the path stop converging and start
ricocheting across the valley.

{{< input name="lr" type="slider" min="0.01" max="0.35" step="0.01" value="0.12" label="step size (η)" >}}
{{< input name="beta" type="slider" min="0" max="0.9" step="0.05" value="0" label="momentum (β)" >}}
{{< input name="steps" type="slider" min="1" max="60" step="1" value="25" label="steps" >}}

```{js}
// A "once" cell: no `//| input:`, so the DAG builds it a single time and never
// tears it down. We read the sliders live with tali.value(), redraw the path in
// place via tali.onInput(), and keep the dragged start point in a local that
// survives every redraw. That is what lets the sliders and the drag coexist.
const NS = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
const W = 660, H = 380, pad = 26;
const xmin = -4, xmax = 4, ymin = -2.6, ymax = 2.6;
const B = 6;                                   // valley anisotropy
const loss = (x, y) => 0.5 * (x * x + B * y * y);
const grad = (x, y) => [x, B * y];             // ∇L = (x, 6y)

// domain <-> pixel maps
const px = (x) => pad + ((x - xmin) / (xmax - xmin)) * (W - 2 * pad);
const py = (y) => H - pad - ((y - ymin) / (ymax - ymin)) * (H - 2 * pad);

const svg = document.createElementNS(NS, "svg");
svg.setAttribute("viewBox", `0 0 ${W} ${H}`);
svg.setAttribute("width", "100%");
svg.setAttribute("role", "img");
svg.setAttribute("aria-label", "A loss surface with a draggable start point and the descent path it produces.");
svg.style.maxWidth = W + "px";
svg.style.height = "auto";
svg.style.touchAction = "none";                // let us own touch-drag
svg.style.overflow = "hidden";                 // clip a divergent path to the box
svg.style.border = "1px solid var(--tali-border)";
svg.style.borderRadius = "8px";
svg.style.background = "var(--tali-bg)";
svg.style.boxSizing = "border-box";            // width:100% must include the border, else 2px overflow

function line(x1, y1, x2, y2, stroke, w, opacity) {
  const l = document.createElementNS(NS, "line");
  l.setAttribute("x1", x1); l.setAttribute("y1", y1);
  l.setAttribute("x2", x2); l.setAttribute("y2", y2);
  l.setAttribute("stroke", stroke); l.setAttribute("stroke-width", w);
  if (opacity != null) l.setAttribute("stroke-opacity", opacity);
  svg.appendChild(l); return l;
}

// axes through the minimum
line(px(xmin), py(0), px(xmax), py(0), "var(--tali-muted)", 1, 0.4);
line(px(0), py(ymin), px(0), py(ymax), "var(--tali-muted)", 1, 0.4);

// iso-loss contours: x^2 + 6y^2 = 2c  ->  ellipse, semi-axes sqrt(2c), sqrt(2c/B)
for (const c of [0.5, 2, 4.5, 8, 12.5]) {
  const ax = Math.sqrt(2 * c), ay = Math.sqrt(2 * c / B);
  const el = document.createElementNS(NS, "ellipse");
  el.setAttribute("cx", px(0)); el.setAttribute("cy", py(0));
  el.setAttribute("rx", (ax / (xmax - xmin)) * (W - 2 * pad));
  el.setAttribute("ry", (ay / (ymax - ymin)) * (H - 2 * pad));
  el.setAttribute("fill", "none");
  el.setAttribute("stroke", "var(--tali-border-strong)");
  el.setAttribute("stroke-opacity", "0.55");
  svg.appendChild(el);
}

// the minimum
const minMark = document.createElementNS(NS, "circle");
minMark.setAttribute("cx", px(0)); minMark.setAttribute("cy", py(0));
minMark.setAttribute("r", 3); minMark.setAttribute("fill", "var(--tali-fg)");
svg.appendChild(minMark);

// the descent path (rebuilt each redraw)
const path = document.createElementNS(NS, "polyline");
path.setAttribute("fill", "none");
path.setAttribute("stroke", "var(--tali-accent)");
path.setAttribute("stroke-width", "2");
path.setAttribute("stroke-linejoin", "round");
svg.appendChild(path);

// the draggable start point
const ball = document.createElementNS(NS, "circle");
ball.setAttribute("r", 7);
ball.setAttribute("fill", "var(--tali-accent)");
ball.setAttribute("stroke", "var(--tali-bg)");
ball.setAttribute("stroke-width", "2");
ball.style.cursor = "grab";
const title = document.createElementNS(NS, "title");
title.textContent = "Drag to move the starting point";
ball.appendChild(title);
svg.appendChild(ball);

const caption = document.createElement("p");
caption.style.margin = "0.4rem 0 0";
caption.style.color = "var(--tali-muted)";
caption.style.fontSize = "0.9rem";

let start = [-3.4, 1.4];                        // drag state, persists across redraws
let alive = true;
if (invalidation) invalidation.then(() => { alive = false; });

function trajectory() {
  const eta = tali.value("lr") ?? 0.12;
  const beta = tali.value("beta") ?? 0;
  const steps = Math.round(tali.value("steps") ?? 25);
  let [x, y] = start, vx = 0, vy = 0;
  const pts = [[x, y]];
  let diverged = false;
  for (let i = 0; i < steps; i++) {
    const [gx, gy] = grad(x, y);
    vx = beta * vx - eta * gx;
    vy = beta * vy - eta * gy;
    x += vx; y += vy;
    if (Math.abs(x) > 40 || Math.abs(y) > 40) { diverged = true; break; }
    pts.push([x, y]);
  }
  return { pts, diverged, end: pts[pts.length - 1] };
}

function redraw() {
  if (!alive) return;                           // gated by invalidation, not DOM-attachment:
  const { pts, diverged, end } = trajectory();  // the first paint runs before tali-js mounts us
  path.setAttribute("points", pts.map(([x, y]) => `${px(x)},${py(y)}`).join(" "));
  ball.setAttribute("cx", px(start[0]));
  ball.setAttribute("cy", py(start[1]));
  const steps = Math.round(tali.value("steps") ?? 25);
  const startLoss = loss(start[0], start[1]);
  const endLoss = loss(end[0], end[1]);
  if (diverged) {
    caption.textContent = "The step size is so large the path flies off the surface — it has diverged.";
  } else if (endLoss > startLoss + 1e-9) {
    caption.textContent = `The loss rose to ${endLoss.toFixed(3)} from ${startLoss.toFixed(3)}: this step size is too large, and the path is climbing out of the valley instead of into it.`;
  } else {
    caption.textContent = `After ${steps} steps the loss fell to ${endLoss.toFixed(3)} (the minimum is 0).`;
  }
}

// drag via pointer capture: no window-level listeners to leak
const clientPt = svg.createSVGPoint();
function toDomain(evt) {
  clientPt.x = evt.clientX; clientPt.y = evt.clientY;
  const p = clientPt.matrixTransform(svg.getScreenCTM().inverse());
  const x = xmin + ((p.x - pad) / (W - 2 * pad)) * (xmax - xmin);
  const y = ymin + ((H - pad - p.y) / (H - 2 * pad)) * (ymax - ymin);
  return [Math.max(xmin, Math.min(xmax, x)), Math.max(ymin, Math.min(ymax, y))];
}
let dragging = false;
ball.addEventListener("pointerdown", (e) => {
  dragging = true; ball.setPointerCapture(e.pointerId);
  ball.style.cursor = "grabbing"; e.preventDefault();
});
ball.addEventListener("pointermove", (e) => {
  if (!dragging) return; start = toDomain(e); redraw();
});
ball.addEventListener("pointerup", (e) => {
  dragging = false; ball.releasePointerCapture(e.pointerId);
  ball.style.cursor = "grab";
});

tali.onInput(["lr", "beta", "steps"], redraw);
redraw();

const wrap = document.createElement("figure");
wrap.style.margin = "0";
wrap.appendChild(svg);
wrap.appendChild(caption);
return wrap;
```

## The slope, written down {#sec-math}

The surface you were just playing on is one tidy formula. Writing $\theta = (x, y)$
for a point on it, the loss is

$$ L(\theta) = \tfrac{1}{2}\!\left(x^2 + 6\,y^2\right). $$

That factor of six is the whole personality of the problem. Move the same small
distance away from the floor along $x$ or along $y$, and the $y$ direction costs six
times as much loss. The result is the long, narrow trough you can see in the rings:
gentle end to end, steep wall to wall.

The direction of steepest **ascent** is the gradient, the vector of partial
derivatives, which for this loss is short enough to read off by hand:

$$ \nabla L(\theta) = \left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial x},\ \frac{\partial L}{\partial y}\right) = (x,\ 6y). $$

Gradient descent simply walks the other way. It takes a fixed fraction $\eta$ of the
gradient, the **step size** (or *learning rate*), and subtracts it:

$$ \theta \;\leftarrow\; \theta - \eta\,\nabla L(\theta). $$

@fig-landscape draws one such step. At a point $\theta$ up on the slope, $-\nabla L$
points inward, toward the floor, and a step of length $\eta\lVert\nabla L\rVert$
carries you part of the way there.

![The loss surface as contour rings: shallow along the valley floor (the $x$ axis), steep across it (the $y$ axis). At a point on the slope, the descent step $-\nabla L$ points inward toward the minimum.](landscape.svg){#fig-landscape}

Here is the catch that the rest of the page is about. The same $\eta$ multiplies both
components of the gradient, but the two components live on very different scales: the
$y$ component is $6y$, the $x$ component only $x$. A step size gentle enough to crawl
along the shallow $x$ direction barely moves; make it big enough to move usefully and
it can overshoot the steep $y$ walls entirely. That tension is exactly what the step
size slider lets you feel.

## One step at a time {#sec-walk}

The playground shows the whole path at once. To see *why* it bends the way it does,
step through the five scenes below: the picture redraws for each one, from the bare
landscape to a full run and, finally, to a run that falls apart.

{{< input name="scene" type="select" options="landscape,gradient,step,iterate,diverge" value="landscape" label="scene" >}}

```{js}
//| input: scene
const NS = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
const W = 460, H = 340, pad = 24;
const xmin = -4, xmax = 4, ymin = -2.6, ymax = 2.6;
const B = 6;
const loss = (x, y) => 0.5 * (x * x + B * y * y);
const grad = (x, y) => [x, B * y];
const px = (x) => pad + ((x - xmin) / (xmax - xmin)) * (W - 2 * pad);
const py = (y) => H - pad - ((y - ymin) / (ymax - ymin)) * (H - 2 * pad);
const scene = tali.value("scene") || "landscape";
const START = [-3.2, 1.6];

const svg = document.createElementNS(NS, "svg");
svg.setAttribute("viewBox", `0 0 ${W} ${H}`);
svg.setAttribute("width", "100%");
svg.setAttribute("role", "img");
svg.setAttribute("aria-label", "Loss surface illustrating scene: " + scene);
svg.style.maxWidth = W + "px";
svg.style.height = "auto";
svg.style.overflow = "hidden";
svg.style.border = "1px solid var(--tali-border)";
svg.style.borderRadius = "8px";
svg.style.background = "var(--tali-bg)";
svg.style.boxSizing = "border-box";            // width:100% must include the border, else 2px overflow

function el(name, attrs) {
  const n = document.createElementNS(NS, name);
  for (const k in attrs) n.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]);
  svg.appendChild(n);
  return n;
}
// axes + contours + minimum, always
el("line", { x1: px(xmin), y1: py(0), x2: px(xmax), y2: py(0), stroke: "var(--tali-muted)", "stroke-width": 1, "stroke-opacity": 0.4 });
el("line", { x1: px(0), y1: py(ymin), x2: px(0), y2: py(ymax), stroke: "var(--tali-muted)", "stroke-width": 1, "stroke-opacity": 0.4 });
for (const c of [0.5, 2, 4.5, 8, 12.5]) {
  const ax = Math.sqrt(2 * c), ay = Math.sqrt(2 * c / B);
  el("ellipse", {
    cx: px(0), cy: py(0),
    rx: (ax / (xmax - xmin)) * (W - 2 * pad),
    ry: (ay / (ymax - ymin)) * (H - 2 * pad),
    fill: "none", stroke: "var(--tali-border-strong)", "stroke-opacity": 0.5,
  });
}
el("circle", { cx: px(0), cy: py(0), r: 3, fill: "var(--tali-fg)" });

function dot(p, color) {
  el("circle", { cx: px(p[0]), cy: py(p[1]), r: 5, fill: color || "var(--tali-accent)", stroke: "var(--tali-bg)", "stroke-width": 1.5 });
}
function arrow(a, b, color) {
  el("line", { x1: px(a[0]), y1: py(a[1]), x2: px(b[0]), y2: py(b[1]), stroke: color, "stroke-width": 2.5, "marker-end": "url(#tip)" });
}
function polyline(pts, color) {
  el("polyline", { points: pts.map(([x, y]) => `${px(x)},${py(y)}`).join(" "), fill: "none", stroke: color, "stroke-width": 2, "stroke-linejoin": "round" });
}
function run(eta, steps, beta) {
  let [x, y] = START, vx = 0, vy = 0;
  const pts = [[x, y]];
  for (let i = 0; i < steps; i++) {
    const [gx, gy] = grad(x, y);
    vx = (beta || 0) * vx - eta * gx; vy = (beta || 0) * vy - eta * gy;
    x += vx; y += vy;
    if (Math.abs(x) > 60 || Math.abs(y) > 60) break;
    pts.push([x, y]);
  }
  return pts;
}
// arrow marker (built namespace-correctly, not via innerHTML)
const defs = document.createElementNS(NS, "defs");
const marker = document.createElementNS(NS, "marker");
for (const [k, v] of Object.entries({ id: "tip", viewBox: "0 0 10 10", refX: 8, refY: 5, markerWidth: 6, markerHeight: 6, orient: "auto-start-reverse" })) marker.setAttribute(k, v);
const tip = document.createElementNS(NS, "path");
tip.setAttribute("d", "M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z");
tip.setAttribute("fill", "currentColor");
marker.appendChild(tip);
defs.appendChild(marker);
svg.insertBefore(defs, svg.firstChild);
svg.style.color = "var(--tali-accent)";

if (scene === "landscape") {
  dot(START);
} else if (scene === "gradient") {
  const [gx, gy] = grad(START[0], START[1]);
  const n = Math.hypot(gx, gy);
  arrow(START, [START[0] - 0.9 * gx / n, START[1] - 0.9 * gy / n], "var(--tali-accent)"); // -grad, downhill
  dot(START);
} else if (scene === "step") {
  const eta = 0.12; const [gx, gy] = grad(START[0], START[1]);
  const next = [START[0] - eta * gx, START[1] - eta * gy];
  polyline([START, next], "var(--tali-accent)");
  dot(START); dot(next, "var(--tali-fg)");
} else if (scene === "iterate") {
  polyline(run(0.12, 30, 0), "var(--tali-accent)");
  dot(START);
} else if (scene === "diverge") {
  polyline(run(0.34, 22, 0), "var(--tali-accent)");
  dot(START);
}
return svg;
```

**The landscape.** The rings are the loss. Descent begins at the marked point, high on
the steep left wall of the valley. Everything that follows is a rule for getting from
there to the dark point in the middle.

**Which way is downhill.** At the start point, the negative gradient $-\nabla L$ points
almost straight *across* the valley, not along it. That is the trap: the steepest
direction is rarely the direction of the minimum.

**One step.** Multiply that direction by the step size and move. With a modest $\eta$
the first step lands well down the wall but overshoots the floor slightly, ending up on
the far side. The path has already started to zig-zag.

**Many steps.** Repeat, and the zig-zags shrink as the walls get shallower near the
floor. The path settles into the trough and crawls the long way toward the minimum. It
works, but notice how much of the effort went sideways.

**Too far.** Now push the step size past the edge. Each step across the valley is longer
than the last; instead of settling, the path climbs the opposite wall higher every time
and ricochets out of the frame. Same rule, same surface, one number too large.

## Choosing the step size {#sec-rate}

Watching the path is one way to judge a step size; watching the **loss fall** is
another, and often clearer. The chart below plots the loss at each iteration for
whatever the three sliders are set to right now. A healthy run drops fast and flattens
near zero; a too-large one bottoms out early and then climbs, the curve turning back
upward as the path escapes the valley.

```{js}
//| input: lr, beta, steps
const B = 6, loss = (x, y) => 0.5 * (x * x + B * y * y), grad = (x, y) => [x, B * y];
const eta = tali.value("lr"), beta = tali.value("beta"), steps = Math.round(tali.value("steps"));
let x = -3.4, y = 1.4, vx = 0, vy = 0;
const data = [{ i: 0, loss: loss(x, y) }];
for (let i = 1; i <= steps; i++) {
  const [gx, gy] = grad(x, y);
  vx = beta * vx - eta * gx; vy = beta * vy - eta * gy;
  x += vx; y += vy;
  data.push({ i, loss: Math.min(loss(x, y), 1e6) });
}
const fig = Plot.plot({
  height: 240,
  marginLeft: 54,
  x: { label: "iteration →" },
  y: { type: "log", label: "↑ loss (log scale)", grid: true },
  marks: [
    Plot.line(data, { x: "i", y: "loss", stroke: "currentColor", strokeWidth: 2 }),
    Plot.dot(data, { x: "i", y: "loss", r: 2, fill: "currentColor" }),
  ],
});
fig.style.color = "var(--tali-accent)";
return fig;
```

::: {.callout-warning title="The step size is a two-sided constraint"}
For a quadratic like this one, plain gradient descent converges only while
$\eta < 2/\lambda_{\max}$, where $\lambda_{\max}$ is the largest curvature, here the
$6$ of the steep direction. That puts the ceiling at $\eta < \tfrac{1}{3}$. Below it,
smaller is safer but slower; above it, no amount of patience helps. Most of the art of
training is keeping $\eta$ under a ceiling you cannot see directly.
:::

## A shove downhill: momentum {#sec-momentum}

The slow part of every run above was the crawl along the valley floor, where the
gradient is tiny and each step barely moves. **Momentum** fixes this by giving the ball
inertia: instead of stepping by the gradient alone, it accumulates a velocity that
carries over from step to step,

$$ v \leftarrow \beta\,v - \eta\,\nabla L(\theta), \qquad \theta \leftarrow \theta + v. $$

The friction term $\beta$ (the momentum slider) decides how much of the past velocity
survives. Along the shallow floor the gradient keeps pointing the same way, so the
velocity builds and the ball rolls faster; across the steep walls the gradient keeps
flipping sign, so those contributions cancel and the wobble is damped. @fig-momentum
shows both runs from the same start for the same number of steps: plain descent is
still short of the minimum while the momentum run has arrived.

![Two descent runs from the same start, fourteen steps each. Plain gradient descent (dashed) is still crawling along the floor; the momentum run (solid) has reached the minimum.](momentum.svg){#fig-momentum}

Scroll back to the playground and raise the momentum slider on a run that was
converging slowly: the same $\eta$ now reaches the floor in far fewer steps. Push it too
high, though, and momentum overshoots on its own, a third way to make the path unstable.

## What to carry away {#sec-takeaways}

::: {.callout-note title="Three things the valley taught us"}
- **Steepest is not shortest.** The negative gradient points across the valley, not at
  the minimum, so descent zig-zags. Curvature, not distance, sets the direction.
- **The step size has a ceiling.** One number, shared across every parameter, has to be
  small enough for the steepest direction and is therefore usually too small for the
  rest. Cross the ceiling and the run diverges no matter what.
- **Momentum buys back the crawl.** Accumulating velocity accelerates the shallow
  directions and cancels the wobble in the steep ones, which is why nearly every modern
  optimizer keeps some form of it.
:::

Every trick that came later, adaptive rates, per-parameter scaling, learning-rate
schedules, is a more careful answer to the same two questions you just felt by hand:
which way, and how far.
